# Pinned toolchain versions for Weave.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for local and CI toolchain versions.
# `make check-prereqs` (and scripts/check-prereqs.sh) enforce these minimums,
# and the CI workflow reads the same values.
#
# Keep in sync when bumping:
#   NODE_VERSION    -> package.json "engines.node", .tool-versions
#   NPM_VERSION     -> package.json "engines.npm"
#   GO_VERSION      -> go.mod "toolchain" directive, .tool-versions
#                      (the go.mod "go" directive stays at 1.25.0, the minimum
#                      language version the dependencies need).
#                      A developer whose base Go is older still satisfies
#                      check-prereqs.sh: it runs `go version` from the
#                      repository root, where the default GOTOOLCHAIN=auto has
#                      already resolved the toolchain directive above, so the
#                      pinned version is what gets reported and used.
#   DOCKER_VERSION  -> documented prerequisite in README.md
#   COMPOSE_VERSION -> documented prerequisite in README.md
#   GOLANGCI_LINT_VERSION
#                   -> installed from source by CI and `make lint-go`, so it is
#                      always compiled with GO_VERSION above. A prebuilt binary
#                      built with an older Go refuses to run against a module
#                      targeting a newer one.
#   GOOSE_VERSION   -> the migration runner, installed from source by
#                      `make migrate-*`. Pinned because a migration runner
#                      decides what runs against a database.
#   SQLC_VERSION    -> generates the database access layer. Pinned because
#                      regenerating with a different version produces a diff
#                      that has nothing to do with the change under review;
#                      CI checks the committed output matches this version.
#   GOVULNCHECK_VERSION, GITLEAKS_VERSION
#                   -> the two security scanners CI runs. Pinned rather than
#                      `latest` so a scan result is reproducible and the tool
#                      that gates the build is a reviewed version, not whatever
#                      was published since the last run. GOVULNCHECK_VERSION
#                      tracks the newest release that builds with GO_VERSION;
#                      v1.8.0 needs go1.26, which the toolchain pin now
#                      satisfies.

NODE_VERSION=23.11.0
NPM_VERSION=10.9.2
GO_VERSION=1.26.8
DOCKER_VERSION=28.1.1
COMPOSE_VERSION=2.35.1
GOLANGCI_LINT_VERSION=2.13.2
GOVULNCHECK_VERSION=1.8.0
GITLEAKS_VERSION=8.30.1
GOOSE_VERSION=3.28.0
SQLC_VERSION=1.31.1
